Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
neuroscientists have always believed  brain implants to be the panacea for all  debilitating injuries today we explore  this obscure medical research field  known as bmi or brain machine interface  scientists have finally been given the  green light to begin human trials to  prove their concept the company that has  received the approval is called syncron  but obviously as a fanboy i couldn't  very well talk about this topic without  discussing elon's neuralink as well so  in this video i'll take a look at both  but before i get on with that let's  first understand what bmi actually is  what is bmi bmi is essentially a direct  communication pathway between the  brain's electrical activity and an  external device this pathway is achieved  by using a brain computer interface or  bci bci is any device that translates  neural information into commands capable  of controlling external software or  hardware such as a communicator or  robotic arm tilted it towards yourself  and sip and coffee from a straw  and that was the first time in nearly 15  years consider bcis as a bridge through  which individuals with motor or sensory  impairments can communicate with the  rest of the world using different types  of devices this technology is often used  as an assisted living device for  paralyzed people so bcis have been  around for decades having first been  researched by jacques vidal at the  university of california los angeles or  ucla in the early 70s they are primarily  of two types non-invasive and invasive  most bcis since their development have  been of the non-invasive type these are  the caps you see being put on people's  heads with electrodes sticking out they  have limitations since our skull is in  the way which blocks the signal's  efficiency the other is the invasive  type which is a lot more efficient but  it literally requires a chip being  inserted inside the brain by cutting out  a portion of the skull scientists have  experimented with this type of bci  mostly on animals however some limited  human experimentation has been done the  first devices implanted in humans  appeared in the mid-1990s this was the  utah array  it's composed of a hundred little micro  needles the utah array has been the  current industry standard for bci  technology since then it is a small  square computer chip with spikes coming  out of it electric array consists of 100  micro electrodes little micro needles  that are about a millimeter and a half  long that project out from a silicon  substrate the needles are extremely  sharp  and so this device can be implanted into  the cerebral cortex or into the  peripheral nervous system  the spikes go into the brain after  cutting out a chunk of the skull and  deliver electrical signals from the  brain to a computer box attached to the  head the computer box then sends the  signals to the intended device even  though it is the standard of the bci  world the utar array is still very  reliant on 90s technology and since its  success there has been very little  movement in the bci industry until a  couple of years ago elon's neuralink so  earlier i mentioned all our favorite  tech billionaire elon in 2016 he founded  neuralink to develop a device that can  be implanted into a human brain and  communicate with a computer to translate  a person's thoughts into action it was  revealed that the company's goal would  be to treat brain related disorders and  try to cure them a pretty ambitious goal  if you ask me but no scientific  discovery would exist if there were no  ambitious people like him so since 2016  neurolink has been developing a fully  implantable wireless high channel count  bci implant that will enable people with  paralysis to directly use their neural  activity to operate computers and mobile  devices despite significant progress it  is not neuralink that has received  approval for human trials but a  competitor company called syncron  instead in all likelihood this is  because of how neuralink works so how  does it work anyway well the device is  implanted by removing some skull  material then electrode wires are  inserted into the outer cortex layer  using a robotic sewing machine the  electrode wires go in a couple of  millimeters enabling the robot to place  the electrode directly onto the neuron  that they want to read this results in  massive amounts of signal coming out of  the brain and clearer communication with  the linked external device the electrode  is so small that the skin can be folded  back and sewn up afterwards even though  this is a super invasive procedure you  can rest assured that you will not be  walking around with any major visible  signs of the implant it may leave a bit  of scarring but soon it'll not be  visible when the hair grows back with  elon involved you know he's going to  push boundaries somehow with neuralink  he intends to create an unprecedented  kind of robotic brain surgery talk about  unprecedented no one has ever had a  robotic sewing machine stitch into a  human brain before but that's what  neuralink will attempt to do recently he  also said that the intention is to have  the entire procedure performed by a  robot  so this is our surgical robot and we  actually ultimately want this robot to  do  uh essentially the entire surgery  so  in everything from from  incision  uh  removing the the skull inserting  electrodes placing the device um and  then um closing things up and having you  ready to leave  there are various advantages to this  robotic surgery the procedure can be  done in less than an hour and without  general anesthesia also the promise  recovery time is fast because patients  don't have to be admitted for it you can  literally walk into the surgery and an  hour later come out as a cyborg with the  ability to control devices with your  thoughts did anyone else get flashes of  the terminator and skynet after hearing  this because i certainly did anyways  looking at what the intention is one  can't help but root for the success of  neuralink although it has not yet  received human trial confirmation  because of this invasive procedure the  good news is that they have already  received its fda breakthrough device  designation the fda is the united states  food and drug administration it is the  agency responsible for protecting public  health by ensuring the safety efficacy  and security of drugs and biological  products among other things the fda's  breakthrough device designation is  granted to novel medical devices that  have the potential to provide effective  treatment to life-threatening or  irreversibly debilitating diseases this  is a major step forward and a great  milestone for neurolink as it means that  the fda recognizes the life-changing  potential of its device but because of  the complexity of its surgical procedure  it is not surprising that neuralink may  need to wait a little longer for human  trials but how is the synchron device  different and why have they received the  green light for human trials over  neuralink synchron stentrode neuralink  is not the only startup exploring bcis  synchron neurolink's biggest competitor  made headway and got fda approval for  limited human trials on six patients in  july of 2021. before that they had  already tested the device on two  patients in australia as well also  founded in 2016 their device the  stentrode is an endovascular brain  implant it is designed to enable  patients to wirelessly control digital  devices through thought and improve  functional independence much like  neuralink's device however when it comes  to getting the implant inside the human  body their procedure is significantly  less invasive than that of neuralink the  stentrode is designed to be implanted  through the patient's vein until it  reaches the brain what we're doing  differently is using the blood vessels  as the natural highway into the brain  and lacing the inside of the blood  vessels with electrodes or sensors that  can record activity from the brain  instead of drilling through the skull  the stentrode is inserted through a  catheter into the patient's jugular vein  the catheter runs all the way up into a  blood vessel that is nestled within the  motor cortex of the brain when the  catheter is pulled back the hollow wire  mesh of the stench road which comprises  of a net-like material with 16 sensors  expands out and interfaces with the  walls of the blood vessel the wire at  the other end of the stentrode is then  connected to a very small computer  device that is implanted inside the  patient's chest cavity those signals  travel through  a lead that exits out the body through a  telemetry unit that goes into a computer  and those signals the implant connects  with the rest of the system via  bluetooth either paired with a computer  or even just a smartphone with synchron  stentrode there are no visible signs  that a patient has an implant in their  brain after the procedure another  advantage is that the surgery can be  done in any hospital setting in a few  hours plus this endovascular procedure  is already used for other surgical  treatments and most surgeons are already  trained in this after receiving fda  approval last year syncron has  successfully implanted the stentrode  device on one patient at the mount sinai  hospital in new york they planned to  expand the program to five more patients  as per the fda approval by the end of  this year if their australian trial  results are anything to go by then this  should be smooth sailing for them these  trials were done in 2019 after synchron  received human trial approval from  australian officials for patients with  multiple sclerosis the results saw no  adverse reactions to the implant even  better they achieved unsupervised home  use of the stentrode device in less than  100 days after the surgery at the  trial's end both participants had used  the device for tasks such as text  messaging online shopping and managing  their finances independently looks like  synchron is giving neuralink a run for  their money let's hope that the  competition helps improve both their  products and ultimately the lives of  those who need them so far the only  downside to the synchron stentrode is  that it is inserted inside a blood  vessel that runs through the brain and  not inside the cortex tissue so it does  not physically interface with the  neurons this means that the signal  released is limited and not as strong as  neuralink signal but on the flip side  the implant is much less invasive than  neuralink implications for neuralink it  is still quite commendable that synchron  has made this much headway neuralink may  lag behind but the approval of syncron's  clinical trial represents a major step  towards clearing regulatory and  practical hurdles for the bci industry  in the long run neurolink will benefit  from this as well the trial's success  will also allow neuralink the  opportunity to learn from stentrode  especially regarding data privacy  concerns hopefully by the time they are  ready for human trials neuralink will  have addressed those issues and any  others that are likely to come up along  the way while many of us may never need  medical bcis i am glad that there are  individuals and companies that are  dedicating their lives to developing  this life-changing technology a future  in which disease and injury do not  dictate an individual's independence and  quality of life is one that i cannot  wait to live in how about you what do  you think of these bcis do you believe  neurolink will get approved for human  trials soon let me know in the comment  section below if you enjoyed this video  hit the subscribe button and continue  watching more videos from this channel  by clicking on one of the cards on your  screen see you at the next one

